WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.490
- We will halve inflation this year

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:04.560
to ease the cost of living

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.870
and give people financial security.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:08.880
- [Narrator] That was UK
Prime Minister Rishi Sunak

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.080
at the beginning of the year

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.850
pledging to cut inflation in half,

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:14.640
which at that time was 10.5%.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.540
Inflation has since fallen
to 8.7%, which is good,

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:19.530
but experts and officials

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:22.860
are more interested in
something else, core inflation,

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.710
which has actually risen
since the start of the year.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.030
What's behind that rise?

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:27.863
And what does it mean

00:00:27.863 --> 00:00:31.110
for Rishi Sunak's big pledge?
(audience applauding)

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:33.120
First, it's important to
understand the difference

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:35.820
between core inflation
and headline inflation.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.280
- Headline inflation is
the classic benchmark

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:39.660
for consumer prices.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.750
It calculates an index
based on stuff that we buy,

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.420
like food, energy, rent, clothes,

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:46.950
and a whole bunch of other stuff.

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.980
Core inflation strips out
the more volatile things

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.090
like food and energy,

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:52.800
and this is the metric
that the Bank of England

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.870
is closely watching right now.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:56.910
- [Narrator] UK headline
inflation is falling,

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:59.430
but at a slower pace than
of a major economies.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:01.980
It's still twice as high as in the US.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.260
The UK's core inflation, meanwhile,

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.330
is traveling in the opposite direction.

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:09.540
It's now at its highest level since 1992,

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:12.720
fueled by higher wages
and corporate profits.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.120
- One of the big drivers
of this is services,

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.700
so that's prices for
things like air travel,

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.610
cultural goods, other kinds of services.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:23.070
This is in contrast with
the US and the Eurozone

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:25.440
where core inflation has
been coming down recently.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.180
In the US, one of the big reasons for this

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:29.970
was the cooling of housing rental prices.

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:31.440
- [Narrator] Persistently
high core inflation

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.170
could make the Prime Minister's
goal harder to achieve.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:37.260
The Treasury Chief says, "The
target of around 5% inflation

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.630
is still possible, but it won't be easy."

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:41.760
- I think the sweet
spot for the UK economy

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.250
in terms of our long-term competitiveness

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:49.250
is if we can do the best of
what European countries do

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:52.020
in terms of investment in skills,

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:54.180
investment in infrastructure,

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.700
but have that US

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.340
dynamism, innovation, spark

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:00.600
- [Narrator] Hunt's desired sweet spot

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:03.000
will in part depend on if
that core inflation rate

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.190
can start to follow the US and Eurozone

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:07.350
in a downward direction.

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:09.540
The government is supported in its efforts

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:10.830
by the Bank of England.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.050
The Central Bank sees
raising interest rates

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:15.240
as its best tool to bring inflation down

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.040
to its own 2% target.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.743
It has been raising the
rate since the end of 2021.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:22.290
This makes borrowing more expensive,

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:24.360
which leads to people
having less money to spend,

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:25.920
which lowers demand on goods,

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.110
and ultimately, slows prices.

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:30.840
- Central Banks have had a
really difficult job recently,

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.300
they've been trying to tame inflation

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.790
with interest rates being their main tool,

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:38.760
but they also have to try
to raise interest rates

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:41.700
without damaging the economy too severely.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:43.770
So it's about finding this
delicate balancing act.

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.650
In the US, we've seen the
Fed pause interest rates

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:49.710
while it's assessing the
impact of his latest hikes,

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:51.690
but it does look like in the UK

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:54.270
because of this
persistently high inflation,

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:55.350
the BOE is unlikely

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:59.034
to be able to pause its
hiking cycle anytime soon.

00:02:59.034 --> 00:03:01.784
(tranquil music)

